.TH SOURCEKNOCK 1 "2026-10-25" "sourceknock 1.0" "sourceknock"
.SH NAME
sourceknock \- source-port port-knocking daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sourceknock
.RI [ CONFIGFILE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sourceknock
is a port-knocking daemon that unlocks a hidden service port by watching the
.B source ports
of incoming packets, rather than the destination ports that
traditional knocking encodes the secret in.
.PP
Standard port knocking puts the knock sequence in the
.I destination
port, so a scanner watching destination ports sees a client stepping through a
fixed list of ports and can fingerprint it.
.B sourceknock
keeps the destination port fixed and varies the
.I source
port instead, so the knock blends in with ordinary traffic.
.PP
How it works:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
The daemon captures packets on an interface using an
.B AF_PACKET
raw socket (requires root or
.B CAP_NET_RAW ).
.IP \(bu 2
For each packet it decodes the source port and source IP from both IPv4 and
IPv6 frames (IPv6 extension headers are walked, bounded to 8 hops).
.IP \(bu 2
A per-client state machine watches the stream of source ports and waits for the
configured ordered sequence to arrive within the timing window.
.IP \(bu 2
On a successful knock the daemon programs
.B nftables
to let that client's source IP reach the hidden service port, then closes it
again after a fixed TTL or on the first connection.
.RE
.PP
.B Threat model.
Source ports are trivially spoofable, like any L4 header field.
This gives
.B sourceknock
stealth and obfuscation (the attack surface stays invisible; noisy scans look
normal) but
.B not
authentication. Rely on your service's own authentication (SSH keys, passwords)
for real security; do not treat
.B sourceknock
as the only control.
.SH ARGUMENTS
The daemon takes a single optional argument: the path to its configuration
file. If omitted it defaults to
.BI /etc/sourceknock/sourceknock.conf .
See
.IR CONFIGURATION
below and the samples in
.BR examples/ .
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is INI-style (knockd-flavoured) with a
.B [global]
section and one
.B [trigger:NAME]
section per hidden service. The minimum per trigger is a
.B port
and a
.BR sequence ;
the fields are:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
.B service
Human-readable name; also used to namespace the nftables table
(``inet sourceknock_<service>'').
.IP \(bu 2
.B port
The hidden service port(s) to open. Accepts a single port, a comma-separated
list, or an inclusive range, e.g.
.BI 22 ,
.BI "80, 443" ,
or
.BI "8000-8002" .
.IP \(bu 2
.B protocol
.BI tcp
or
.BI udp .
.IP \(bu 2
.B sequence
The ordered source-port knock, e.g.
.BI 7000,8000,9000 .
.IP \(bu 2
.B window
Seconds within which the whole sequence must complete (default 5).
.IP \(bu 2
.B mode
One of
.BI fixed_ttl ,
.BI close_on_connect ,
or
.BI permanent .
.IP \(bu 2
.B duration
Seconds the port stays open with
.BI fixed_ttl
(default 30).
.IP \(bu 2
.B fallback_ttl
Safety maximum open time for
.BI close_on_connect
so a client that never connects cannot leave the port open forever (default 300).
.IP \(bu 2
.B admin_sequence
Optional revoke knock for
.BI permanent
mode; when it completes the client's IP is removed and the port closes again.
.RE
.SH MODES
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
.B fixed_ttl \- keep the port open for
.BI duration
seconds, then close.
.IP \(bu 2
.B close_on_connect \- close as soon as the client makes a connection, with
.BI fallback_ttl
as a safety net.
.IP
For TCP the first packet to a service port (the connection's SYN) closes the
port. For UDP there is no handshake, so the first subsequent UDP packet to a
service port closes it; the arming knock itself does not close the port the
instant it opens.
.IP \(bu 2
.B permanent \- keep the port open with no timeout. Pair with
.BI admin_sequence
to revoke.
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Open SSH (port 22) for 30 seconds after a knock:
.IP
.BR "sudo sourceknock /etc/sourceknock/sourceknock.conf" ""
.PP
Knock from a client by sending each probe with the right SOURCE port (7000,
8000, 9000) toward the fixed service port — the destination port is
irrelevant — then connect:
.IP
.nf
# hping3 -D sets the SOURCE port; the knock lives there, not the dest port
for p in 7000 8000 9000; do
    hping3 -D $p --udp -i u1000 server </dev/null 2>/dev/null &
done
wait
ssh -p 22 user@server
.fi
.PP
Run under systemd (installs the binary and units, then reloads systemd):
.IP
.nf
sudo make install
sudo useradd --system --user-group --home-dir /var/lib/sourceknock \
    --no-create-home sourceknock
sudo systemctl enable --now sourceknock
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.BI /etc/sourceknock/sourceknock.conf
Default configuration file.
.TP
.BI /usr/local/bin/sourceknock
Installed daemon binary (see
.BR make install ).
.TP
.BI /etc/systemd/system/sourceknock.service
Default systemd unit.
.TP
.BI /etc/systemd/system/sourceknock@.service
Per-instance systemd template unit.
.SH DEBUGGING
To inspect what the daemon has programmed, list the nftables set for a
service. With a trigger named
.BI ssh :
.IP
.BR "nft list set inet sourceknock_ssh allowed_v4" ""
.PP
Common problems (permissions, missing/old nftables, IPv6 quirks) are covered in
the project's
.B CONTRIBUTING.md
and the Troubleshooting section of the README.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
.B 0
The daemon ran until signalled.
.IP \(bu 2
.B 1
Configuration error, reward/state-machine setup failure, or capture open
failure (usually missing privileges).
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (8),
.BR nft (8),
.BR hping3 (8)
.PP
